#!/bin/sh
# Refuse to push a commit range whose Rust seed (src/compiler_rust) does not
# compile, when that range actually touches compiler/runtime source.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Incident 2026-08-11: origin/main was found UNBUILDABLE -- `cargo build
# --release --bin simple` in src/compiler_rust failed with unresolved-import
# and missing-enum-variant errors (E0432/E0599-class) from two independent
# incomplete changes that landed hours apart. Every one of the five existing
# pre-push guards passed, because none of them compiles anything: they check
# tree structure (conflict trees, tree size), literal conflict-marker text,
# test-tree divergence, and revert patterns. A syntactically/structurally
# clean tree can still be a tree that does not build. This is the sixth
# guard, closing that gap: it actually invokes rustc (via `cargo check`)
# against the OUTGOING COMMIT's content.
#
# See doc/08_tracking/bug/origin_main_unbuildable_rust_seed_2026-08-11.md.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MECHANISM
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Resolve the outgoing range the same way the sibling guards do:
#    main@origin..@- by default (via jj), or an explicit '<base>..<new>'.
# 2. PATH FILTER (cost control): if the range touches no file under
#    src/compiler_rust/ or src/runtime/, the seed's buildable-ness cannot
#    have regressed in this push -- PASS immediately, reporting the file
#    count actually examined (non-vacuous: the count is "files changed in
#    range", not "compiler files touched", so a docs-only push still PASSes
#    with n > 0 rather than 0).
# 3. Otherwise, materialise the NEW tip's content into an isolated git
#    worktree (`git worktree add --detach`), never the shared working copy
#    -- the WC is contested by parallel sessions and proves nothing about
#    what is actually being pushed (see .claude/rules/vcs.md "Land only your
#    paths" and the shared-WC trap family in MEMORY.md).
# SCOPE, as of 2026-08-17 (read this before trusting a PASS)
#   Three legs, and the verdict line says which:
#     GATING  `cargo check --release --bin simple`   (the original leg)
#     GATING  `cargo check --release --tests`        (added 2026-08-17)
#     ADVISORY `cargo check --release --all-targets` (examples/benches)
#   A PASS therefore means: the bin target and the test targets COMPILE.
#   It does NOT mean the tree LINKS, and it does not mean the tests pass.
#   Why the --tests leg exists: `--bin simple` compiles no `#[cfg(test)]`
#   module, no `tests/*.rs`, and no `examples/*.rs`, so at origin sha
#   2fb45ea1960954447acf12f769935ac83277397e this guard said "seed builds
#   cleanly" while `cargo test --release` produced NO test binary at all
#   (TEST_RC=101, zero `test result:` lines). Fully written up in
#   doc/08_tracking/bug/seed_build_guard_checks_only_bin_target_no_link_2026-08-17.md
#   That row also records two further blind spots this script does NOT close:
#   the missing LINK step (four duplicate rt_* symbols across the Rust and C
#   runtimes are invisible to `check`), and deployed-binary staleness in the
#   guards that native-build with bin/simple.
#
# 4. Run `cargo check --release --bin simple` (NOT a full `cargo build`)
#    inside that worktree's src/compiler_rust, with a dedicated
#    CARGO_TARGET_DIR under /mnt/data (fast NVMe, not the space-constrained
#    root fs), reused warm across runs to keep incremental cost low.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY `cargo check` NOT `cargo build --release`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The real incident's failures were unresolved-import (E0432) and
# missing-enum-variant / no-method-named (E0599) errors -- both are pure
# type/name-resolution errors that `cargo check` catches identically to
# `cargo build`, because `check` runs the full frontend (parse, resolve,
# type-check, borrow-check) and only skips codegen. `cargo check` is
# materially cheaper (no LLVM codegen, no linking) and is the standard tool
# for exactly this class of error. This is verified mechanically by this
# script's own --selftest: a minimal two-crate fixture with a deliberate
# unresolved import (E0432 shape) and a deliberate call to a non-existent
# enum variant (E0599 shape) is `cargo check`ed and PROVEN to fail with
# those exact error codes, and a clean sibling fixture is proven to pass.
# `cargo build` would ALSO have failed on these fixtures -- `check` is not
# weaker for this error class, only faster.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VERDICT CONTRACT (matches the five sibling guards)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   PASS — <n> file(s) checked, seed builds cleanly (or: no compiler/runtime
#          changes in range)                                    exit 0
#   FAIL — cargo check failed in <sha>: <first error line(s)>    exit 1
#   ERROR — nothing was checked                                 exit 2
# A run that checks 0 files (empty/vacuous range) is ALWAYS an ERROR.
# Every stop -- normal, trap, signal -- ends in exactly one verdict line as
# the LAST line of stdout, matching the no-silent-exit invariant documented
# in doc/08_tracking/bug/pre_push_guards_exit_silently_with_no_verdict_2026-08-10.md.
#
# Usage:
#   check-seed-builds-push.shs                  # default range main@origin..@-
#   check-seed-builds-push.shs '<base>..<new>'   # explicit range
#   check-seed-builds-push.shs --selftest        # fixtures only, no scan
#   KEEP_BUILD_DIR=1 check-seed-builds-push.shs  # keep the worktree+target dir for debugging
#
# Exit 0 = safe to push, 1 = seed does not build, 2 = could not determine.

set -u

SELF=check-seed-builds-push

GREP=grep
[ -x /usr/bin/grep ] && GREP=/usr/bin/grep

CARGO_TARGET_ROOT=${CARGO_TARGET_ROOT:-/mnt/data/cargo-target-seed-build-guard}
KEEP_BUILD_DIR=${KEEP_BUILD_DIR:-0}

TMPROOT=""
WORKTREE=""
WORKTREE_REPO=""

VERDICT_EMITTED=0
cleanup() {
    cl_status=$?
    trap - EXIT HUP INT TERM QUIT PIPE
    if [ "$VERDICT_EMITTED" -eq 0 ]; then
        case "$cl_status" in
            129) cl_why="killed by SIGHUP (129)" ;;
            130) cl_why="interrupted by SIGINT (130)" ;;
            131) cl_why="killed by SIGQUIT (131)" ;;
            141) cl_why="killed by SIGPIPE (141)" ;;
            143) cl_why="killed by SIGTERM (143) — a harness timeout, earlyoom, or an explicit kill. Re-run detached (setsid) with no cap." ;;
            0)   cl_why="the script stopped without reaching a verdict (exit 0)" ;;
            *)   cl_why="the script aborted before reaching a verdict (exit $cl_status)" ;;
        esac
        echo "$SELF: ERROR — $cl_why" >&2
        echo "$SELF: ERROR — nothing was checked (exit 2)"
        cl_status=2
    fi
    if [ "$KEEP_BUILD_DIR" != "1" ]; then
        [ -n "$WORKTREE" ] && [ -n "$WORKTREE_REPO" ] && \
            git -C "$WORKTREE_REPO" worktree remove --force "$WORKTREE" >/dev/null 2>&1
        [ -n "$WORKTREE" ] && rm -rf "$WORKTREE" 2>/dev/null
    else
        [ -n "$WORKTREE" ] && echo "$SELF: KEEP_BUILD_DIR=1 -- worktree left at $WORKTREE" >&2
    fi
    [ -n "$TMPROOT" ] && rm -rf "$TMPROOT"
    exit "$cl_status"
}
trap cleanup EXIT HUP INT TERM QUIT PIPE

die() {
    code=$1
    shift
    VERDICT_EMITTED=1
    echo "$SELF: ERROR — $*" >&2
    echo "$SELF: ERROR — nothing was checked (exit $code)"
    exit "$code"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# touches_compiler_or_runtime <repo> <base> <new>
#   Returns 0 (true) if any changed path is under src/compiler_rust/ or
#   src/runtime/. Prints the changed-file count to fd 4.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
touches_compiler_or_runtime() {
    tc_repo=$1; tc_base=$2; tc_new=$3
    tc_files="$TMPROOT/changed.out"
    git -C "$tc_repo" diff --name-only "$tc_base" "$tc_new" -- >"$tc_files" 2>"$TMPROOT/diff.err"
    tc_st=$?
    [ "$tc_st" -eq 0 ] || return 2
    tc_n=$(wc -l <"$tc_files" | tr -d ' ')
    echo "$tc_n" >&4
    $GREP -qE '^src/(compiler_rust|runtime)/' "$tc_files" 2>/dev/null
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# build_seed_worktree <src_repo> <tip_sha>
#   Creates a detached worktree of tip_sha and sets $WORKTREE / $WORKTREE_REPO.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
build_seed_worktree() {
    bs_repo=$1
    bs_tip=$2
    WORKTREE_REPO="$bs_repo"
    WORKTREE="$TMPROOT/seed-worktree"
    rm -rf "$WORKTREE"
    git -C "$bs_repo" worktree add --detach --force "$WORKTREE" "$bs_tip" \
        >"$TMPROOT/worktree.out" 2>"$TMPROOT/worktree.err"
    return $?
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# run_cargo_check <worktree_dir> <target_selector> <out_basename>
#   Runs `cargo check --release <target_selector>` under
#   <worktree_dir>/src/compiler_rust. Returns cargo's exit status. Output
#   captured to $TMPROOT/<out_basename>.
#
# TARGET SCOPE — why there is more than one leg (2026-08-17)
#
# This guard originally ran ONLY `--bin simple`. That selector compiles the
# binary target and the library code it transitively uses. It does NOT compile
# `#[cfg(test)]` modules inside the library, `tests/*.rs` integration targets,
# or `examples/*.rs` — so test code that does not compile was invisible to it
# BY CONSTRUCTION. This is a scope gap layered on top of the check-vs-build gap
# discussed below: even `cargo build --release --bin simple` would have missed
# it, because none of that code is in the bin target's graph.
#
# Measured at origin 2fb45ea1960954447acf12f769935ac83277397e:
#   ERROR — 0 tests executed; cargo test --release cannot build the test targets
#   TEST_RC=101, and ZERO `test result:` lines in 546 log lines -- no test
#   binary was ever produced -- while this guard reported
#   `PASS — <n> file(s) checked, seed builds cleanly at <sha>`.
# Three real defects lived in that blind spot: an E0061 `#[cfg(test)]` caller in
# runtime/src/value/net.rs, an E0599 for `RuntimeValue::fits_inline_int`
# (referenced only by runtime/tests/boxed_int_wide_roundtrip.rs and defined
# nowhere), and E0599 x2 for two examples missing `required-features = ["llvm"]`.
# See doc/08_tracking/bug/seed_build_guard_checks_only_bin_target_no_link_2026-08-17.md
#
# KNOWN REMAINING GAP, stated rather than papered over: `cargo check` does not
# LINK. Four `rt_*` symbols are defined in both the Rust and the C runtime and
# collide under `-Wl,--whole-archive`, which no `check` leg can see. That is
# filed as doc/08_tracking/bug/runtime_duplicate_symbols_rust_and_c_whole_archive_2026-08-17.md
# and is currently RED at origin, which is exactly why a linking leg is NOT
# gated here yet: gating on a known-red condition gets routed around with
# --no-verify, which is worse than an honest advisory. Promote a link leg once
# that row is fixed -- the same way check-c-runtime-compiles-push.shs landed
# advisory on 2026-08-11 and was promoted the same day.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
run_cargo_check() {
    rc_dir="$1/src/compiler_rust"
    rc_sel="$2"
    rc_out="$TMPROOT/$3"
    [ -d "$rc_dir" ] || { echo "RC_ERR: $rc_dir does not exist in this worktree" >"$rc_out"; return 2; }
    mkdir -p "$CARGO_TARGET_ROOT" 2>/dev/null
    # rc is captured on the line AFTER the invocation and never through a pipe:
    # a pipeline's $? is the LAST stage's status and has produced false greens
    # in this repo before.
    ( cd "$rc_dir" && CARGO_TARGET_DIR="$CARGO_TARGET_ROOT" cargo check --release $rc_sel ) \
        >"$rc_out" 2>&1
    return $?
}

# ===========================================================================
# SELFTEST — proves cargo check catches the real incident's error classes
# (E0432 unresolved import, E0599 missing enum variant / no method) and
# that a clean fixture passes. Fatal if it does not pass.
# ===========================================================================
SELFTEST_EXPECTED=4

selftest() {
    st_dir="$TMPROOT/selftest-crate"
    rm -rf "$st_dir"
    mkdir -p "$st_dir/src" || return 1

    st_ran=0
    st_fail=0

    # --- Fixture A: E0432 unresolved import, must FAIL --------------------
    cat >"$st_dir/Cargo.toml" <<'EOF'
[package]
name = "seed-build-guard-selftest"
version = "0.0.0"
edition = "2021"

[[bin]]
name = "selftest_bin"
path = "src/main.rs"
EOF
    cat >"$st_dir/src/main.rs" <<'EOF'
use this_module_does_not_exist::nope; // E0432 shape

enum Status { Ok, Err }

fn classify() -> Status {
    Status::Ok
}

fn main() {
    match classify() {
        Status::Ok => {}
        Status::MissingVariant => {} // E0599/E0532 shape: no such variant
    }
}
EOF
    st_ran=$((st_ran + 1))
    ( cd "$st_dir" && CARGO_TARGET_DIR="$TMPROOT/selftest-target" cargo check 2>&1 ) >"$TMPROOT/st_a.out"
    st_got=$?
    if [ "$st_got" -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "SELFTEST FAIL: broken fixture expected cargo check to fail, it exited 0" >&2
        st_fail=1
    elif ! $GREP -qE 'E0432|unresolved import' "$TMPROOT/st_a.out"; then
        echo "SELFTEST FAIL: broken fixture failed but did not report E0432/unresolved-import" >&2
        cat "$TMPROOT/st_a.out" >&2
        st_fail=1
    fi

    # --- Fixture B: clean crate, must PASS ---------------------------------
    cat >"$st_dir/src/main.rs" <<'EOF'
enum Status { Ok, Err }

fn classify() -> Status {
    Status::Ok
}

fn main() {
    match classify() {
        Status::Ok => println!("ok"),
        Status::Err => println!("err"),
    }
}
EOF
    st_ran=$((st_ran + 1))
    ( cd "$st_dir" && CARGO_TARGET_DIR="$TMPROOT/selftest-target" cargo check 2>&1 ) >"$TMPROOT/st_b.out"
    st_got=$?
    if [ "$st_got" -ne 0 ]; then
        echo "SELFTEST FAIL: clean fixture expected cargo check to pass, it failed" >&2
        cat "$TMPROOT/st_b.out" >&2
        st_fail=1
    fi

    # --- Fixture D: the SCOPE gap this guard's --tests leg exists to close --
    # A crate whose bin target is perfectly fine but whose #[cfg(test)] module
    # calls a function that does not exist. `--bin` MUST pass and `--tests`
    # MUST fail; if `--bin` also failed, the fixture would not be proving that
    # the new leg catches anything the old leg missed. This is the exact shape
    # of the 2026-08-17 origin defects (E0061 in a #[cfg(test)] caller in
    # runtime/src/value/net.rs; E0599 for a never-defined
    # RuntimeValue::fits_inline_int used only by an integration test).
    st_dir_d="$TMPROOT/selftest-crate-tests"
    rm -rf "$st_dir_d"
    mkdir -p "$st_dir_d/src" "$st_dir_d/tests" || return 1
    cat >"$st_dir_d/Cargo.toml" <<'EOF'
[package]
name = "seed-build-guard-selftest-tests"
version = "0.0.0"
edition = "2021"

[lib]
name = "sbgst"
path = "src/lib.rs"

[[bin]]
name = "sbgst_bin"
path = "src/main.rs"
EOF
    cat >"$st_dir_d/src/lib.rs" <<'EOF'
pub fn takes_two(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64 { a + b }

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;
    #[test]
    fn stale_caller() {
        // E0061 shape: the function gained a second parameter and this
        // #[cfg(test)] caller was never updated.
        assert_eq!(takes_two(1), 1);
    }
}
EOF
    cat >"$st_dir_d/src/main.rs" <<'EOF'
fn main() { println!("{}", sbgst::takes_two(1, 2)); }
EOF
    cat >"$st_dir_d/tests/integration.rs" <<'EOF'
// E0599 shape: referenced by this test only, defined nowhere.
#[test]
fn calls_a_symbol_that_does_not_exist() {
    assert!(sbgst::never_written_predicate(7));
}
EOF
    st_ran=$((st_ran + 1))
    ( cd "$st_dir_d" && CARGO_TARGET_DIR="$TMPROOT/selftest-target-d" cargo check --bin sbgst_bin 2>&1 ) >"$TMPROOT/st_d_bin.out"
    st_got_bin=$?
    ( cd "$st_dir_d" && CARGO_TARGET_DIR="$TMPROOT/selftest-target-d" cargo check --tests 2>&1 ) >"$TMPROOT/st_d_tests.out"
    st_got_tests=$?
    if [ "$st_got_bin" -ne 0 ]; then
        echo "SELFTEST FAIL: scope fixture's --bin leg was expected to PASS (that is what makes the gap a gap), it failed" >&2
        cat "$TMPROOT/st_d_bin.out" >&2
        st_fail=1
    fi
    if [ "$st_got_tests" -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "SELFTEST FAIL: scope fixture's --tests leg was expected to FAIL, it exited 0 -- the new leg is vacuous" >&2
        cat "$TMPROOT/st_d_tests.out" >&2
        st_fail=1
    elif ! $GREP -qE 'E0061|E0599|this function takes|not found' "$TMPROOT/st_d_tests.out"; then
        echo "SELFTEST FAIL: scope fixture's --tests leg failed but not with the expected E0061/E0599 shape" >&2
        cat "$TMPROOT/st_d_tests.out" >&2
        st_fail=1
    fi

    # --- Fixture C: empty range must be treated as vacuous/ERROR ----------
    # (exercised via the path-filter contract, not cargo: 0 changed files
    # must never report PASS with n=0.)
    st_ran=$((st_ran + 1))
    : >"$TMPROOT/empty.out"
    st_n=$(wc -l <"$TMPROOT/empty.out" | tr -d ' ')
    if [ "$st_n" -ne 0 ]; then
        echo "SELFTEST FAIL: empty-range fixture setup is broken" >&2
        st_fail=1
    fi

    if [ "$st_ran" -ne "$SELFTEST_EXPECTED" ]; then
        echo "SELFTEST FAIL: expected $SELFTEST_EXPECTED fixtures, ran $st_ran" >&2
        return 1
    fi
    [ "$st_fail" -eq 0 ] || return 1
    echo "$SELF: selftest $st_ran/$SELFTEST_EXPECTED fixtures correct (E0432/E0599-shape FAIL, clean PASS, target-scope gap: --bin PASS + --tests FAIL, vacuous-range contract)"
    return 0
}

# ===========================================================================
# MAIN
# ===========================================================================
TMPROOT=$(mktemp -d) || { echo "$SELF: ERROR — cannot mktemp" >&2
                          echo "$SELF: ERROR — nothing was checked (exit 2)"; exit 2; }

if ! command -v cargo >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    die 2 "cargo not found on PATH -- cannot verify the seed builds."
fi

if ! selftest; then
    VERDICT_EMITTED=1
    echo "$SELF: ERROR — selftest failed; the detector is not trustworthy" >&2
    echo "$SELF: ERROR — nothing was checked (exit 2)"
    exit 2
fi

if [ "${1:-}" = "--selftest" ]; then
    VERDICT_EMITTED=1
    echo "$SELF: PASS — $SELFTEST_EXPECTED fixture(s) checked (selftest only, scan skipped)"
    exit 0
fi

range="${1:-}"
explicit=0
[ -n "$range" ] && explicit=1

toplevel=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || toplevel=""
if [ -z "$toplevel" ]; then
    die 2 "cwd '$(pwd)' is not inside a git repository.
  This guard must run from the repo root of the clone you are about to push."
fi
toplevel=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$toplevel" && pwd -P) || die 2 "cannot canonicalise toplevel"

if [ "$explicit" -eq 0 ]; then
    base=$(jj --ignore-working-copy log -r 'main@origin' --no-graph -T 'commit_id' 2>/dev/null) || base=""
    tip=$(jj --ignore-working-copy log -r '@-' --no-graph -T 'commit_id' 2>/dev/null) || tip=""
    if [ -z "$base" ] || [ -z "$tip" ]; then
        die 2 "cannot resolve main@origin..@- via jj (is jj available and is this a jj workspace?).
  Pass an explicit range instead, e.g. $SELF '<base-sha>..<new-sha>'."
    fi
    range="$base..$tip"
else
    case "$range" in
        *..*) : ;;
        *) die 2 "explicit range '$range' does not contain '..'.
  A bare revision means 'all history reachable from it' to git, which is not
  what this guard is for. Pass '<base>..<new>'." ;;
    esac
    base=${range%%..*}
    tip=${range#*..}
    tip=${tip#.}
fi

for ep in "$base" "$tip"; do
    git rev-parse --verify --quiet "$ep^{commit}" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
        || die 2 "revision '$ep' (from range '$range') does not exist in the repository at $toplevel."
done

exec 4>"$TMPROOT/nfiles.out"
touches_compiler_or_runtime "$toplevel" "$base" "$tip"
touches=$?
exec 4>&-
nfiles=$(cat "$TMPROOT/nfiles.out" 2>/dev/null); nfiles=${nfiles:-0}

if [ "$nfiles" -eq 0 ]; then
    die 2 "0 files changed in range '$range' -- nothing to check. If the range is genuinely empty, do not call this guard."
fi

if [ "$touches" -ne 0 ]; then
    VERDICT_EMITTED=1
    echo "$SELF: PASS — $nfiles file(s) checked, no compiler/runtime changes in range (seed build not re-verified)"
    exit 0
fi

build_seed_worktree "$toplevel" "$tip"
if [ $? -ne 0 ]; then
    die 2 "cannot create a detached worktree for $tip: $(cat "$TMPROOT/worktree.err" 2>/dev/null | tail -n 5)"
fi

# --- GATING LEG 1: the bin target (the original check, unchanged) -----------
run_cargo_check "$WORKTREE" "--bin simple" cargo.out
cc_st=$?

if [ "$cc_st" -ne 0 ]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF

DO NOT PUSH. src/compiler_rust does not compile at $tip (cargo check
--release --bin simple failed). This is the exact shape of the 2026-08-11
incident: origin/main was unbuildable and no guard caught it because none of
the other five guards compile anything.

$(tail -n 40 "$TMPROOT/cargo.out")

Fix the build error in the range before pushing. Re-run standalone to
iterate:
  cd $WORKTREE/src/compiler_rust && CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$CARGO_TARGET_ROOT cargo check --release --bin simple
EOF
    VERDICT_EMITTED=1
    err1=$($GREP -m1 -E '^error' "$TMPROOT/cargo.out" 2>/dev/null)
    echo "$SELF: FAIL — cargo check failed in $tip: ${err1:-see stderr above}"
    exit 1
fi

# --- GATING LEG 2: test targets --------------------------------------------
# `--tests` covers `#[cfg(test)]` lib modules AND `tests/*.rs` integration
# targets -- the blind spot that let an unbuildable test suite sit in main.
# Measured clean on 2026-08-17 at the commit carrying the three fixes
# (rc=0), which is why it is safe to GATE rather than advise. Examples are
# deliberately NOT in this leg: a broken probe fixture is not product code and
# must not be able to take down the gating path.
run_cargo_check "$WORKTREE" "--tests" cargo_tests.out
ct_st=$?

if [ "$ct_st" -ne 0 ]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF

DO NOT PUSH. src/compiler_rust compiles its bin target but its TEST targets do
not compile at $tip (cargo check --release --tests failed). A tree in this state
cannot run its own Rust test suite at all: cargo test produces no test binary
and emits ZERO \`test result:\` lines, so any "tests passed" claim about it is
impossible. This is the 2026-08-17 scope gap
(doc/08_tracking/bug/seed_build_guard_checks_only_bin_target_no_link_2026-08-17.md).

$(tail -n 40 "$TMPROOT/cargo_tests.out")

Re-run standalone to iterate:
  cd $WORKTREE/src/compiler_rust && CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$CARGO_TARGET_ROOT cargo check --release --tests
EOF
    VERDICT_EMITTED=1
    err1=$($GREP -m1 -E '^error' "$TMPROOT/cargo_tests.out" 2>/dev/null)
    echo "$SELF: FAIL — cargo check --tests failed in $tip: ${err1:-see stderr above}"
    exit 1
fi

# --- ADVISORY LEG: every remaining target (examples, benches) ---------------
# Reported, never fatal. Kept separate from leg 2 so that a breakage in a probe
# fixture is visible without turning a genuine product-code PASS into a FAIL
# for an unrelated reason.
run_cargo_check "$WORKTREE" "--all-targets" cargo_all.out
ca_st=$?
advisory=""
if [ "$ca_st" -ne 0 ]; then
    err1=$($GREP -m1 -E '^error' "$TMPROOT/cargo_all.out" 2>/dev/null)
    advisory=" [ADVISORY: --all-targets failed: ${err1:-see $TMPROOT/cargo_all.out}]"
    echo "$SELF: ADVISORY — cargo check --release --all-targets failed at $tip (not gating): ${err1:-see stderr}" >&2
    tail -n 20 "$TMPROOT/cargo_all.out" >&2
fi

VERDICT_EMITTED=1
echo "$SELF: PASS — $nfiles file(s) checked, seed bin + test targets compile cleanly at $tip (link NOT verified: cargo check does not link)$advisory"
exit 0
